---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import XmlParserTool from '../components/XmlParserTool';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { getTool, getRelatedTools } from '../data/tools';

const tool = getTool('xml-parser')!;
const related = getRelatedTools(tool);

const faqItems = [
  { q: 'What does this show that the XML Formatter doesn\'t?', a: 'It shows the parsed structure as a JSON-like collapsible tree, rather than reformatted XML text — useful for understanding a document\'s shape at a glance.' },
  { q: 'How are attributes shown in the tree?', a: 'Prefixed with @, alongside the element\'s other keys, so you can tell attributes and child elements apart.' },
  { q: 'Is my XML uploaded anywhere?', a: 'No — parsing runs entirely in your browser via the native DOMParser.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout tool={tool}>
  <XmlParserTool client:load />

  <AdSlot placement="below-tool" />

  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[860px] space-y-8 px-4 py-10">
    <RelatedTools tools={related} />

    <details class="rounded-lg border" style="border-color:var(--border);background:var(--bg-subtle)">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--fg)">Documentation</summary>
      <div class="space-y-5 px-4 pb-5 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--fg-muted)">
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">What is XML Parser?</h2>
          <p>XML Parser reads an XML document and renders it as a collapsible, searchable tree — the same interactive JsonTree view used by the JSON tools — so you can explore a document's shape without reading raw angle brackets. It's built for inspection, not for producing a converted file.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">How it works</h2>
          <p>Input is parsed with the browser's native <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">DOMParser</code> in <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">application/xml</code> mode. If a <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">parsererror</code> node appears in the result, its message is shown in the error bar and, when the browser's error text includes a line number, that line is highlighted in the input gutter. On success, the document's root element is walked recursively: each attribute becomes a key prefixed with <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">@</code>, repeated sibling tags are grouped into arrays, and an element with no child elements collapses to its plain text content (or an object with an <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">@attr</code>/<code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">#text</code> shape if it also has attributes or text). The resulting object is handed to the tree view for display — the tool never generates a text output panel.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">Features</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
            <li>Collapsible, searchable tree view of the parsed structure</li>
            <li>Attributes shown as @-prefixed keys alongside child elements</li>
            <li>Repeated sibling tags automatically grouped into arrays</li>
            <li>Line-highlighted parse errors in the input editor</li>
            <li>Load a .xml file directly from disk</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">Example</h2>
          <p>Input: <code class="font-mono text-xs" style="color:var(--accent)">{'<tools><tool id="1">json-formatter</tool><tool id="2">yaml-formatter</tool></tools>'}</code></p>
          <p>Tree view shows (conceptually, as the underlying object):</p>
          <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded border p-3 font-mono text-xs" style="border-color:var(--border);background:var(--bg)">{`{
  "tools": {
    "tool": [
      { "@id": "1", "#text": "json-formatter" },
      { "@id": "2", "#text": "yaml-formatter" }
    ]
  }
}`}</pre>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">Common errors</h2>
          <p>The most common failure is more than one root element, or a tag that's opened but never closed — both trip the DOMParser's parsererror and prevent a tree from rendering at all. Unlike a schema validator, this tool only checks well-formedness as a side effect of parsing; it doesn't verify tags against an XSD or DTD.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2 class="mb-1.5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--accent)">Best practices</h2>
          <p>Use this tool when you need to understand an unfamiliar XML document's shape — which elements repeat, which carry attributes — before writing code against it. If you actually need the data as a JSON file to consume elsewhere, use XML to JSON instead; this tool's tree view isn't downloadable.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </details>

    <FAQ items={faqItems} />
  </div>
</ToolLayout>
